window.KINTSU_DOCS_INDEX = [{"page":"docs.html","title":"Kintsu documentation","heading":"","id":"","text":"Install, keys, configuration, questions, roadmap. One page each."},{"page":"docs.html","title":"Kintsu documentation","heading":"Design documents","id":"design","text":"In the repository. They are the reference if this site and the code disagree. VISION.md — The problem, the existing tools, the roadmap ARCHITECTURE.md — Entities, use cases, adapters DAEMON.md — The resident process MODELS.md — Several models, one per task UI.md — The bubble CONTRIBUTING.md — How to contribute"},{"page":"install.html","title":"Install","heading":"","id":"","text":"One static binary. No runtime, no account."},{"page":"install.html","title":"Install","heading":"One line","id":"one-line","text":"macOS · Linux · zsh, bash, fish today Installs the binary in ~/.local/bin and asks before adding one line to your shell configuration. Open a new shell. Done."},{"page":"install.html","title":"Install","heading":"If you skipped the hook","id":"hook","text":"Add the line yourself, then open a new shell. zsh bash fish ~/.zshrc ~/.bashrc In bash, messages arrive at the next prompt and there is no ghost text. Everything else is the same. ~/.config/fish/config.fish"},{"page":"install.html","title":"Install","heading":"Other ways","id":"other","text":"Prebuilt binaries arrive with the first tagged release. Building from source works today. macOS Linux From source Servers, tmux, Herdr prebuilt binary with v0.1 Intel Macs: x86_64-apple-darwin . prebuilt binary with v0.1 Static build, any distribution. aarch64 too. cargo, from the repository today Needs a Rust toolchai"},{"page":"install.html","title":"Install","heading":"Uninstall","id":"uninstall","text":"removes the binary and the hook line Nothing else was touched."},{"page":"install.html","title":"Install","heading":"Installer options","id":"options","text":"For automation, a custom directory, or a shell you manage yourself. --dir DIR install directory; default ~/.local/bin , or $KINTSU_INSTALL_DIR --no-hook do not edit any shell configuration file --no-model do not install Ollama or pull qwen2.5-coder:7b ; also KINTSU_NO_MODEL=1 --yes answer yes to every question: the hoo"},{"page":"keys.html","title":"Keys and commands","heading":"","id":"","text":"Every action has a key, a clickable word and a command. The single letters work once the panel is open."},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"","id":"","text":"~/.config/kintsu/config.toml : write it with kintsu setup or start from kintsu default-config , check it with kintsu doctor . This page is the target schema. Today the binary reads models , routing , quiet , ui and capture ( sources , max_lines ) and ignores the rest, so a file written for the full schema loads; every change is listed in the changelog."},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Models","id":"models","text":"A model is a named endpoint: a provider, a model id, how to reach it, what it may be used for. Name them the way you will read them in kintsu doctor : tiny , haiku , local-big , work . [models.<name>] provider covers needs where ollama any model Ollama serves; health and listing through the native API Ollama running lo"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Routing","id":"routing","text":"Five tasks. For each, an ordered list of model names. The router takes the first candidate the constraints allow and that is not in a failed state (circuit breaker); on a timeout or an error it moves to the next. rules-only is a pseudo-model meaning \"do without a model\" and every list ends with it whether you write it "},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Constraints","id":"constraints","text":"Constraints filter the candidate lists before the router selects one. They keep a case containing a secret away from cloud models and enforce the budget. [routing.constraints] constraint value effect sensitive_output \"local_only\" · \"ask\" · \"allow\" default local_only . ask shows the Privacy tab before any cloud call. of"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Keys","id":"keys","text":"A key source, per model, in order of preference. The daemon reads keys once, keeps them in memory, never logs them, never puts them in the protocol. keychain environment variable command literal key = { … } macOS Keychain or Linux secret-service, service kintsu , account the model's name. Store it with security add-gen"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Agents","id":"agents","text":"Presets define how the brief is passed to each CLI. A template covers any other command. {brief} is the path of the Markdown brief Kintsu wrote; {brief_text} inlines it. [models.<agent>] preset runs claude claude \"$(cat {brief})\" , or claude -p when open_in = \"print\" codex codex \"$(cat {brief})\" , or codex exec for pri"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"The bubble","id":"ui","text":"[ui]"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Noise","id":"noise","text":"[quiet] At the prompt: kintsu ignore with --command , --dir , --session or --always ; kintsu mute 1h ; KINTSU_DISABLE=1 to disable the hook in one shell."},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Daemon and hooks","id":"daemon","text":"[daemon]"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Per project","id":"project","text":"A .kintsu.toml at the root of a repository overrides the user file for cases whose cwd is inside it. Anything can be overridden; these are the ones that make sense to commit. .kintsu.toml"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Complete examples","id":"examples","text":"Local only One key Your subscription At work local only: Ollama, nothing is sent local one key: Haiku for explanations, tiny for classification cloud local no key at all: your Claude Code subscription your account at work: an approved endpoint, private paths, a budget cloud"},{"page":"configuration.html","title":"Configuration","heading":"Commands that touch the configuration","id":"commands","text":"kintsu doctor parses the file, checks the hook, each model's program or key, and the routing names; prints what works and what is missing kintsu default-config prints the commented default file kintsu config path where the configuration and the state files are"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"","id":"","text":"Twenty-four questions in five groups. Missing one? Open an issue ."},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Safety and privacy","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"Does Kintsu ever run a command? No. Never. A fix is inserted in your line editor; you press Enter. In the panel it takes two Enters on purpose: one to insert, one to run. Rules can be marked auto by you, one by one, never by default. The only thing that runs on its own is a read-only probe attached to a rule, such as l"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Does Kintsu ever run a command?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"No. Never. A fix is inserted in your line editor; you press Enter. In the panel it takes two Enters on purpose: one to insert, one to run. Rules can be marked auto by you, one by one, never by default. The only thing that runs on its own is"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"What exactly is sent, and when?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"Nothing until you pick an action that needs a model. Then the redacted case: the command line, exit status and duration, the captured output, the working directory, the git branch and whether it is dirty, the last ten commands with their st"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"What if there is a secret in the output?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"Redaction runs before anything is sent and is on by default: API keys, bearer tokens, JWTs, passwords in URLs, private keys, plus your own patterns. When it finds one, the case is marked sensitive and the router refuses every cloud candidat"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Can I use it fully offline?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"Yes. Rules need nothing. With Ollama, classification, fixes and explanations run on your machine; set offline = \"local_only\" or simply configure no cloud model. Without Ollama you keep the rules and the hand-off to a CLI agent, and Kintsu p"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Does it keep logs? Telemetry?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"No telemetry, no account, no server of ours, ever. Locally, Kintsu keeps small files under ~/.local/state/kintsu : the last commands of each shell, the last failure, the ignore list. Cases are stored as typed, like your shell history; secre"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Can a web page with a kintsu:// link do something to my shell?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"No. A click can only open or focus the panel on an action; it can never insert a command, run one, or start an agent. Case ids are random 128-bit tokens that expire with the case, the socket is a local Unix socket with the peer's uid checke"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Which shells?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"zsh, bash and fish, on Linux and macOS. zsh and fish support ghost-text fixes and messages that arrive while you type. bash has no preexec and no safe way to draw while readline waits, so messages come at the next prompt and there is no gho"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Which terminals make the bubble clickable?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"Any terminal that renders OSC 8 hyperlinks: iTerm2, WezTerm, Kitty, Ghostty, foot, VS Code, Windows Terminal, GNOME Terminal and the other VTE terminals. Alacritty through its hints. Terminal.app does not render links, so the words show but"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Does it work over SSH, inside tmux, inside Herdr?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"Yes. The daemon runs per user on whatever host the shell is on, so on a server it runs on the server. In tmux and Herdr, Kintsu reads the output with capture-pane and pane read ; WezTerm, Kitty and iTerm2 expose their scrollback through the"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Will it slow down my prompt?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"On success, the hook assigns a variable and returns: under a millisecond, no process. On failure, the client queries the daemon with a 40 ms budget and prints the toast if a rule answered in time; otherwise it returns and the bubble arrives"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Does it change my exit status or my history?","id":"safety-and-privacy","text":"No. The hooks return the status of your command so your prompt and your $? are untouched, and they never write to your history. The dependency tests and the hook tests in the repository check both."},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Models and cost","id":"models-and-cost","text":"What does an answer cost? Rules and the tiny local model: nothing. An explanation from a small cloud model such as Haiku: about a tenth of a cent for a typical case. A large model: a few cents. Your agent: whatever your subscription already covers. A daily cap, max_daily_cost , is planned. Can I use my Claude, ChatGPT "},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"What does an answer cost?","id":"models-and-cost","text":"Rules and the tiny local model: nothing. An explanation from a small cloud model such as Haiku: about a tenth of a cent for a typical case. A large model: a few cents. Your agent: whatever your subscription already covers. A daily cap, max_"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Can I use my Claude, ChatGPT or Copilot subscription instead of an API key?","id":"models-and-cost","text":"Yes, through the CLI you already have: Claude Code, Codex, Copilot CLI or Gemini CLI configured as a cli_agent . Kintsu writes the brief and launches the CLI; the CLI uses its own login. That is how Agent works, and it can also serve Why an"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Do I need Ollama?","id":"models-and-cost","text":"No, but it makes a noticeable difference. With a tiny local model, classification and summaries are free and instant, and sensitive cases still get an answer. Without it, those tasks fall back to rules and the toast is generated from rules "},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"How does the router pick a model?","id":"models-and-cost","text":"Per task, an ordered list of candidates from your config. The router drops what the constraints forbid (a cloud model for a sensitive case, a paid model past the budget, a slow model for a fast task), skips a model after three consecutive f"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Which providers?","id":"models-and-cost","text":"Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, Ollama, and any endpoint implementing the OpenAI chat API: OpenRouter, Groq, Mistral, Together, LM Studio, llama-server, vLLM. Plus any CLI agent as a provider. Keys come from your keychain, an environment variabl"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Compared to","id":"compared-to","text":"Isn't this Warp? Warp did it first, and did it well, inside Warp: with a Warp account, Warp's models and Warp's quota. Kintsu does it in the terminal and shell you already use, with any model or agent, keys in your keychain and the source on GitHub. If Warp suits you, keep it. Isn't this thefuck? For chapter 1, yes, an"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Isn't this Warp?","id":"compared-to","text":"Warp did it first, and did it well, inside Warp: with a Warp account, Warp's models and Warp's quota. Kintsu does it in the terminal and shell you already use, with any model or agent, keys in your keychain and the source on GitHub. If Warp"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Isn't this thefuck?","id":"compared-to","text":"For chapter 1, yes, and Kintsu ports its best rules with credit. thefuck waits for you to type a word, has no model for what rules cannot know, cannot read the output, and cannot hand a case to an agent. It also starts a Python interpreter "},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Isn't this Claude Code, Codex, Copilot CLI, Gemini CLI?","id":"compared-to","text":"Those are agents you start yourself. Kintsu detects the failure, writes the brief and launches them with the case attached. They are not competitors; they are where Agent hands off to."},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"What about zsh-autofix, ai-fix, butterfish, shell_gpt?","id":"compared-to","text":"Each covers part of it: zsh-autofix triggers on failure but is zsh plus oh-my-zsh plus Ollama only; ai-fix re-runs your failed command to capture its error, which is unsafe; butterfish wraps your shell in a PTY; shell_gpt and aichat are cha"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"The project","id":"the-project","text":"What works today? The hooks, and a placeholder line after a failure. The bubble, the daemon, the rules and the models are designed in four documents and come with v0.1. The roadmap shows what becomes real when, chapter by chapter. Why Rust, and why one binary? The fast path runs after every failed command and must star"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"What works today?","id":"the-project","text":"The hooks, and a placeholder line after a failure. The bubble, the daemon, the rules and the models are designed in four documents and come with v0.1. The roadmap shows what becomes real when, chapter by chapter."},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Why Rust, and why one binary?","id":"the-project","text":"The fast path runs after every failed command and must start in well under a millisecond; a static binary installs everywhere, including a server you do not own. One crate, three rings as folders, a test that enforces the dependency rule. I"},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"License, contributions?","id":"the-project","text":"MIT. Issues and pull requests are welcome; the most useful contribution before v0.1 is an opinion on the design documents. Conventions are in CONTRIBUTING.md ."},{"page":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","heading":"Why \"Kintsu\"?","id":"the-project","text":"Kintsugi, 金継ぎ, is the Japanese craft of repairing broken pottery with gold. The name says what the tool does: it repairs failed commands, visibly."},{"page":"roadmap.html","title":"Roadmap","heading":"","id":"","text":"No dates. A version ships when every item in its list works."},{"page":"roadmap.html","title":"Roadmap","heading":"Hooks and design","id":"the-skeleton","text":"now  Hooks for zsh, bash and fish Design documents: architecture, daemon, models, UI The installer and this site v0.1 The bubble Goal: a suggestion is shown under every failed command. Daemon: one process per user, Unix socket, started on demand Rule engine: command typos, wrong directory, git, Docker not running, port"}];
